  "title": "Whistle fits multilingual speech recognition into 16.9 MB",
  "narration": "Cactus Compute squeezed multilingual speech recognition into 16.9 megabytes. It is called Whistle, and it runs entirely on-device. No GPU, cloud round trip, or dramatic server invoice. It handles seven languages and 30 seconds per pass. It also returns word timestamps and speech embeddings. The same CPU runtime powers Cactus's Needle model. Devices can run speech and text models in one engine. That matters for wearables, robots, cars, and tiny computers. Audio can stay on the device. The model is Apache-2.0 and ships as one .cact file. Creator Radar surfaced this before the broader feeds did. Sixteen point nine megabytes is suspiciously little luggage.",
